  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Ramp Alternative",
    "body": "Choosing a Ramp alternative starts with deciding whether your business needs a corporate card, a complete commercial checking account, or dedicated accounts payable software. Ramp bundles physical and virtual corporate cards, invoice processing, travel booking, expense tracking and accounts payable bill payment. Alternatives separate these tasks across different pricing models.\n\nFirst, check how each provider charges for user access. Ramp Free allows unlimited cards and expense management for $0 per user per month, while Ramp Plus charges $15 per user per month plus a platform fee based on team size. Brex offers its Essentials tier at $0 per user per month and charges $12 per user per month for Brex Premium. Expensify charges $5 per member per month on Collect. In contrast, Mercury charges flat monthly account prices rather than per-user rates: Mercury base costs $0, Mercury Plus costs $29.90 per month billed annually ($35 per month month-to-month), and Mercury Pro costs $299 per month billed annually ($350 per month month-to-month). Relay Starter charges $0 per month and includes unlimited user seats.\n\nSecond, compare payment execution fees. Ramp provides free wires and same-day ACH payments through its Bill Pay tools. Mercury includes free Automated Clearing House transfers, domestic and international wire transfers in United States dollars (USD), and check mailing on its $0 base plan. Relay states on its pricing page that it saves businesses up to 50% over competitors on wire and same-day ACH payments. BILL lists transaction fees of $0.59 and $1.99 per payment, with the $1.99 fee applying when BILL prints and mails a paper check.\n\nThird, examine cash yields and account balances. Ramp lists a footnoted 4.61% variable yield in a Ramp Investment Account with no minimum deposits or transfer caps, alongside 2% on cash in an FDIC-insured checking account. Relay Starter provides a 1.19% annual percentage yield (APY) on savings, Relay Grow lists 1.87% APY, and Relay Scale lists 3.21% APY. Brex and Expensify do not publish checking account interest yields on their main pricing pages.\n\nRun through these five concrete checks before switching:\n\n- How many employees need a card or an expense login, and does the plan charge per seat or a flat monthly account fee?\n- Does your company send domestic wire transfers or paper checks every month, and what does each provider charge per transaction?\n- Does your accounting workflow require QuickBooks Online, Xero, NetSuite or Sage Intacct?\n- Do you need an integrated commercial checking account, or do you prefer corporate cards linked to an existing external bank account?\n- Does your finance team require multi-entity consolidation, custom user roles or procurement add-ons?"
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Brex Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Brex provides corporate cards, global expense controls, travel booking and spend management. Brex LLC is a wholly owned subsidiary of Capital One, N.A. Brex divides its software into Essentials, Premium and Enterprise tiers. Brex Essentials costs $0 per user per month and covers global card acceptance, local currency wire transfers, accounting integrations and free travel booking. Brex Premium costs $12 per user per month and adds multiple customizable expense policies, enterprise resource planning (ERP) integrations and human resources information system (HRIS) integrations. Brex Enterprise uses custom pricing.\n\nBrex fits businesses that need corporate cards with global acceptance, travel booking and customizable expense policies.\n\nVersus Ramp, Brex falls short on accounting software access for entry-level enterprise integrations. Ramp Plus integrates with NetSuite and Sage Intacct for $15 per user per month plus a team-size platform fee, whereas Brex includes advanced ERP connections in Brex Premium at $12 per user per month or custom Enterprise plans. Ramp also lists a 4.61% variable yield in a Ramp Investment Account and 2% on cash in an FDIC-insured checking account, which Brex's pricing page does not match with a published cash rate.\n\nWorked cost example: For a 10-user team, Brex Essentials costs $0 per year ($0 x 10 x 12). On Brex Premium, that 10-user team pays $12 per user per month, which totals $120 per month or $1,440 per year ($12 x 10 x 12). On Ramp Plus, 10 users cost $150 per month ($15 x 10), which totals $1,800 per year before adding Ramp's unpublished platform fee based on team size. Ramp Free costs $0 for the same 10 users. These figures are arithmetic on list prices for a 10-user example, not quotes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Mercury Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Mercury is a financial technology company offering business banking, company cards and spend management tools. Mercury's pricing page defaults to annual billing with a 15% discount. The Mercury base plan costs $0 per month with no monthly service fees and no minimum balance rules. It includes company cards, expense management, free ACH payments, free checks and free wires in USD. Mercury Plus costs $29.90 per month billed annually, or $35 per month on month-to-month billing. Mercury Plus adds a 30-day free trial and invoicing with ACH debit collection at $1 per transaction. Mercury Pro costs $299 per month billed annually, or $350 per month on month-to-month billing, and reduces ACH debit invoicing to $0 per transaction. Optional add-ons include Mercury Books at $35 per month and Mercury Payroll at $100 per month plus $50 per person paid per month, with both add-on fees waived until December 31, 2026.\n\nMercury fits businesses that want commercial checking, employee cards and free USD wire transfers under a flat monthly plan rather than per-seat software pricing.\n\nVersus Ramp, Mercury falls short on procurement and standalone spend management depth. Ramp provides travel booking, accounts payable bill pay by ACH, card, check and wire, unlimited cards, and invoice creation on its $0 Free tier. Ramp Plus adds procurement as an add-on, multi-entity support and AI expense reviews. Mercury integrates company spend cards directly into its bank accounts, but requires Mercury Plus ($29.90 per month billed annually or $35 monthly) or Mercury Pro ($299 per month billed annually or $350 monthly) to unlock advanced invoicing tools.\n\nWorked cost example: A company with 10 team members using Mercury base pays $0 per year in software maintenance fees. On Mercury Plus, the annual cost is $358.80 billed annually ($29.90 x 12), or $420 per year when paid month-to-month ($35 x 12). On Mercury Pro, the cost is $3,588 per year billed annually ($299 x 12), or $4,200 per year paid month-to-month ($350 x 12). On Ramp Plus, 10 users cost $1,800 per year ($15 x 10 x 12) before Ramp's unpublished team-size platform fee, while Ramp Free costs $0. These figures are arithmetic on list prices for a 10-user example, not quotes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Relay Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Relay provides business checking and savings accounts with integrated payment tools. Relay Starter costs $0 per month and includes unlimited user seats, 1.19% APY on savings (footnoted on its pricing page), and 1% cash back on credit cards (footnoted). Relay also offers Grow and Scale tiers with free trials. Relay Grow provides 1.87% APY on savings and 1.25% cash back on credit cards. Relay Scale provides 3.21% APY on savings and 1.5% cash back on credit cards. Relay Grow and Scale monthly prices are shown on Relay's pricing page. Relay states that it saves customers up to 50% over competitors on wire transfers and same-day ACH payments.\n\nRelay fits small business owners who want checking accounts with unlimited user seats, savings interest and credit card cash back.\n\nVersus Ramp, Relay falls short on corporate spend management workflows. Ramp provides unlimited corporate cards, travel and expense tracking, bill payment by card, check, wire and ACH, invoice creation, and accounts receivable tools on its $0 Free plan. Relay focuses on business checking, multiple deposit accounts, and payment execution, while charging separate transaction fees where noted on its pricing page. Ramp also lists a 4.61% variable yield in a Ramp Investment Account, which sits above Relay Scale's 3.21% savings APY.\n\nWorked cost example: A 10-person business on Relay Starter pays $0 per month in software subscription fees, totaling $0 per year. That same 10-person business pays $0 per year on Ramp Free. If the team upgrades to Ramp Plus, the subscription cost is $1,800 per year ($15 x 10 x 12) before Ramp's team-size platform fee. Relay Grow and Scale monthly prices are shown on Relay's pricing page, so full annual totals for those tiers are omitted here. These figures are arithmetic on list prices for a 10-user example, not quotes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Expensify Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Expensify provides expense tracking, receipt management and employee reimbursement software. Expensify offers three primary service levels: Submit, Collect and Control. Expensify Submit is a free tier that covers receipt SmartScan, mileage tracking and basic expense reports. Expensify Collect costs $5 per member per month and adds automated expense approvals, corporate card reconciliation, and accounting sync. Expensify Control uses custom pricing, listed on its pricing page as low as $9 per member per month for advanced policy enforcement and multi-level approvals. Expensify includes a 30-day free trial.\n\nExpensify fits businesses and traveling employees whose primary requirement is scanning paper receipts, tracking vehicle mileage and submitting expense reports.\n\nVersus Ramp, Expensify falls short on native banking and accounts payable payment breadth. Expensify charges $5 per member per month on Collect for automated expense management and card reconciliation. Ramp includes unlimited corporate cards, accounts payable bill pay by ACH, card, check and wire, invoice creation, and QuickBooks Online and Xero integrations on its $0 Free plan. Expensify does not provide checking accounts or cash interest yields on its pricing page, whereas Ramp lists 2% on cash in an FDIC-insured checking account and 4.61% in a Ramp Investment Account.\n\nWorked cost example: A team of 10 employees on Expensify Collect pays $5 per member per month, which equals $50 per month or $600 per year ($5 x 10 x 12). On Expensify Control at the listed starting rate of $9 per member per month, 10 employees cost $90 per month or $1,080 per year ($9 x 10 x 12). On Ramp Free, that same 10-person team pays $0 per year. On Ramp Plus, 10 users pay $1,800 per year ($15 x 10 x 12) before Ramp's unpublished team-size platform fee. These figures are arithmetic on list prices for a 10-user example, not quotes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "BILL Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "BILL specializes in financial operations software covering accounts payable, accounts receivable and business credit lines. BILL offers four service tiers: Essentials, Team, Corporate and Enterprise, with custom pricing on Enterprise. BILL publishes plan prices on its pricing page. BILL advertises payment processing fees of $0.59 and $1.99 per payment, with the $1.99 fee applying when BILL prints and mails a check. It also lists $1.99 per 1099 filing and commercial credit lines ranging from $1,000 to $5M.\n\nBILL fits companies that prioritize automated bill approvals, check printing and mailing, 1099 tax filings, and access to commercial lines of credit up to $5M.\n\nVersus Ramp, BILL falls short on all-in-one corporate card bundling. Ramp includes corporate cards with unlimited cards, travel and expense management, invoice creation, accounts receivable tools, and free wires and same-day ACH via Bill Pay on its $0 Free tier. BILL applies transaction fees of $0.59 and $1.99 per bill payment, and $1.99 per 1099 filing. BILL publishes subscription prices on its pricing page rather than offering a perpetual $0 spend management software tier.\n\nWorked cost example: Because BILL publishes subscription prices on its pricing page rather than on the summary fact sheet checked here, monthly subscription totals for BILL plans are omitted. On transaction fees alone, a business issuing 50 check payments through BILL pays $99.50 in mailing fees (50 x $1.99) and $19.90 for 10 annual 1099 filings (10 x $1.99). Ramp includes free checks, wires and same-day ACH inside its Bill Pay feature on its $0 Free tier."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "Ramp and its alternatives do not suit every business profile. Review these exclusions before making an operational change:\n\n- Sole proprietors and informal freelancers fit poorly with Ramp and Brex, because both platforms require registered corporate or limited liability business structures to issue corporate charge cards.\n- Companies that handle routine physical cash deposits cannot use Ramp, Brex or Mercury, as none of these fintech platforms operate physical bank branch teller networks.\n- Businesses that require published, low-cost enterprise accounting connections without paying per-seat software fees fit poorly with Ramp Plus, which charges $15 per user per month plus an unpublished team-size platform fee for NetSuite and Sage Intacct integrations.\n- Teams seeking free travel booking with no software subscription fee should note that Brex Essentials includes free travel booking at $0 per user per month, while Expensify Collect charges $5 per member per month.\n- Organizations needing commercial borrowing lines exceeding $5M cannot obtain that financing through BILL, which advertises credit lines up to $5M."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer",
      "body": "The comparison on this page reflects published pricing and features checked on November 26, 2026. Several potential moves by these providers would alter how they compare:\n\n- If Ramp introduces mandatory software subscription fees on its Free plan or removes free wire transfers from Bill Pay, its cost advantage over Expensify and Brex would decrease.\n- If Ramp publishes specific dollar rates for its team-size platform fee on Ramp Plus, annual budget comparisons against Brex Premium could be calculated precisely.\n- If Brex raises its $12 per user per month price on Brex Premium or adds booking fees to travel on Brex Essentials, its competitive standing would shift.\n- If Mercury ends its fee waivers for Mercury Books ($35 per month) and Mercury Payroll ($100 per month plus $50 per person) after December 31, 2026, the cost of running full back-office operations on Mercury will rise by at least $1,620 per year.\n- If Relay publishes lower transaction costs or alters its savings yields from 1.19% on Starter, 1.87% on Grow, and 3.21% on Scale, deposit yield calculations would need revision.\n- If BILL removes its $0.59 and $1.99 payment fees or publishes lower subscription tiers, its accounts payable cost comparison against Ramp Bill Pay would shift."
    }
